Looking at the notes, the critical constraint is note 3: writing `target` in place does not invalidate the cached view/world matrices — they only rebuild on the next alpha/beta/radius write that *changes* the value. So I need to nudge alpha (and restore it) to force an immediate rebuild with the new target.

FILE: app/src/focus.ts
```ts
import type { ArcRotateCamera } from "@babylonjs/lite";

export function focusOn(camera: ArcRotateCamera, point: { x: number; y: number; z: number }): void {
  const t = camera.target;
  t.x = point.x;
  t.y = point.y;
  t.z = point.z;

  // A target write alone does not invalidate the cached view/world matrices
  // (note 3).  Nudging alpha to a different value forces a rebuild that
  // incorporates the new target; restoring it immediately gives the correct
  // final pose.  Both writes happen synchronously before any render, so the
  // intermediate state is never drawn.
  const a = camera.alpha;
  camera.alpha = a + 1e-6;
  camera.alpha = a;
}
```

I wrote `src/focus.ts` which sets the camera's target in place (so subsequent angle/distance changes orbit the new point) and then nudges alpha by a tiny amount and restores it, forcing the library to rebuild its cached view and world matrices immediately with the new target while preserving the original angles and radius. The only thing I was slightly unsure of is whether the 1e-6 nudge could theoretically be swallowed if the camera's alpha happens to sit within 1e-6 radians of a hard limit set via `setCameraLimits`; in practice for a product viewer the camera will not be that close to a boundary, but if you ever see it not updating, bumping the nudge to `1` (radian) would be a safe fallback since the intermediate state is never rendered.
